---
/**
 * ReviewStrip — the ready-to-review queue under `/learn`'s page head (spec §8
 * "M8.2 adds: a ready-to-review strip directly under the page head — at most two
 * cards, zero DOM when empty, never any 'overdue'/countdown vocabulary"; design
 * in `docs/m8-gamification.md`).
 *
 * No props: the lessons it schedules over are the ones `/learn` already injects
 * for its progress island (`[data-lessons]` on the page head), so the strip
 * reads the same build-time list every other surface on the page does and there
 * is no second list to fall out of step. Placement follows from the same
 * attribute — the strip is inserted immediately after that element.
 *
 * THIS IS THE ONLY SURFACE IN THE PRODUCT THAT EVER PROMPTS THE READER. Every
 * other progress display is passive. That is why the design caps it at two
 * cards, why the copy is an invitation rather than an obligation, and why it
 * carries no count of days waited, no lateness, no countdown, no badge, no
 * `role="alert"` and no announcement: it is quiet furniture that happens to be
 * clickable. All of its words live in `REVIEW_COPY` (`src/lib/progress.ts`) so
 * the vocabulary ban is a unit test rather than a review habit.
 *
 * WHY IT RENDERS NOTHING SERVER-SIDE, NOT EVEN A SHELL: "empty ⇒ zero DOM" is a
 * tested calm invariant, not a preference. A reader with nothing due must not
 * meet an empty state, an "all caught up" badge, or a hidden container that
 * quietly tells them a queue exists — nothing here shames absence, and the
 * cheapest way to guarantee that is to have no markup to hide. Due-ness is a
 * question about `localStorage` anyway, which only the client can answer, so the
 * island below BUILDS the strip when there is something to offer and otherwise
 * leaves the page exactly as it shipped. The strip is structurally invisible
 * until a first `practicedAt` exists, which is what makes week one pure learning.
 *
 * WHY THE STYLES ARE `is:global`: Astro scopes a component's CSS by stamping
 * `data-astro-cid-*` onto the elements in ITS TEMPLATE, and this component
 * deliberately has none, so a scoped rule would match nothing it draws. The
 * Visualizer's predict strip is in the same position — its rules "only ever
 * paint something a script created" — and they live in an `is:global` block
 * too. Every selector here is therefore namespaced under `.review-strip`, and
 * the card composes the shared `.track-card` recipe (global.css) rather than
 * restating a lookalike, so the elevation ladder and its hover/pressed steps
 * stay defined once for both card families. Nothing
 * here declares `border-color`, `background-color` or `box-shadow`: this block
 * is unlayered and would beat `@layer components`' state rules from the resting
 * state — the trap `LessonCard` documents.
 *
 * M8.3 — THE NOTE REPLAY. Where the reader left an Explain-it-back note on a due
 * lesson, the card is followed by their own sentence: "You wrote last time: …".
 * That replay IS the mechanic (elaborative interrogation pays off when you meet
 * your earlier explanation again and can check whether you still agree), and it
 * costs the queue nothing — the note is read from the same `progress:v1:{slug}`
 * record the schedule is already derived from, so no card, no key and no request
 * is added. A lesson with no note is offered in exactly the words it was before.
 *
 * The quote sits OUTSIDE the `<a>`, in the card's list item. A 280-character
 * sentence inside the link would become part of its accessible name, which is
 * how a "Binary Search — quick check" link turns into a paragraph a screen-reader
 * user has to sit through before they can decide to follow it.
 *
 * A11Y: a real `<section>` named by a real `<h2>`, so the strip is a landmark a
 * screen-reader user can skip past, sitting at the same heading level as the
 * track sections it precedes. The cards are ordinary links — the whole card is
 * the `<a>`, and its visible text is its accessible name (never an `aria-label`,
 * which would replace it). The arrow is `aria-hidden` decoration and no state is
 * carried by colour.
 */
---

<noscript>
  <style>
    /* Kill-switch, as every M8 component ships one. Belt-and-braces here: the
       strip only ever exists because the island created it, so a JS-off page
       cannot show one. The rule is what makes that structural fact
       unbreakable if the strip is ever server-rendered — and `!important`
       because a kill-switch must be unbeatable by any equal-specificity rule in
       the bundle (QA DEFECT-1). */
    .review-strip {
      display: none !important;
    }
  </style>
</noscript>

<style is:global>
  /* Sits between the page head and the first track section, closer to the head
     than the tracks are to each other: it belongs to the page's opening, not to
     Foundations. */
  .review-strip {
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  /* Deliberately quieter than the track h2s below it (--text-2xl/bold): an
     invitation should not out-shout the curriculum. */
  .review-strip__heading {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  .review-strip__note {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  /* Capped at the prose measure rather than stretched across the 3-column
     lesson grid: two cards spanning the full width would read as the page's
     main event, which they are not. */
  .review-strip__list {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .review-strip__list {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  /* Layout only — the fill, border, radius, shadow and every state come from
     `.track-card`. Padding is denser than that recipe's --space-6 for the same
     reason `.lesson-card` overrides it: these are small cards. */
  .review-strip__card {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    height: 100%;
    padding: var(--space-4);
  }

  .review-strip__text {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-0-5);
  }

  .review-strip__lesson {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  /* States the size of the ask, in the muted role the estimate takes on a
     lesson card. It is an estimate, never a timer: nothing here counts down. */
  .review-strip__check {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  /* The same "this card is a link" convention the lesson and track cards use. */
  .review-strip__arrow {
    margin-left: auto;
    color: var(--brand);
  }

  /* The reader's own earlier words, under the card they belong to: the lesson
     body's blockquote idiom (a left keyline, muted, small), so it reads as a
     quotation rather than as something this page is telling them. Clamped to
     three lines — the whole note is one tap away in the box that wrote it, and a
     280-character wall would out-weigh the invitation it sits under. */
  .review-strip__wrote {
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
    display: -webkit-box;
    -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
    -webkit-line-clamp: 3;
    line-clamp: 3;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* On paper the page is a curriculum listing; a device-local invitation to
     come back has no meaning there (the same reason `.whats-next` is dropped). */
  @media print {
    .review-strip {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  /**
   * The review strip's island: derive what is due, build the strip, insert it.
   *
   * Every rule about WHAT is due lives in `src/lib/progress.ts` — the intervals,
   * the clamp, the two-card cap and the words — so this file only ever decides
   * where the nodes go. Deriving on each render (rather than storing a queue) is
   * what makes two open tabs impossible to disagree: neither of them wrote one.
   */
  import {
    onRestore,
    parseLessonRefs,
    PROGRESS_RESET_EVENT,
    readNote,
    REVIEW_COPY,
    reviewHref,
    selectDueReviews,
    type LessonRef,
  } from '../lib/progress';

  /** Links the section to its heading; one strip per page, so one id is enough. */
  const HEADING_ID = 'review-strip-heading';

  /**
   * The slugs currently drawn, so an unchanged queue is left alone.
   *
   * Not an optimisation: the repaint hooks below fire on a page that is already
   * live and may hold focus, and replacing a card with an identical new node
   * would drop that focus onto `<body>` mid-keyboard-navigation.
   */
  let rendered: string | null = null;

  /**
   * One card: the whole thing is the link, its text is its accessible name —
   * followed, where the reader left one, by their own note as a sibling of that
   * link rather than a child of it (see the note-replay paragraph above).
   */
  function card(lesson: LessonRef): HTMLLIElement {
    const item = document.createElement('li');

    const link = document.createElement('a');
    link.className = 'track-card review-strip__card';
    link.href = reviewHref(lesson.slug);
    link.setAttribute('data-review-card', lesson.slug);

    const text = document.createElement('span');
    text.className = 'review-strip__text';

    const title = document.createElement('span');
    title.className = 'review-strip__lesson';
    title.textContent = lesson.title;

    const check = document.createElement('span');
    check.className = 'review-strip__check';
    check.textContent = REVIEW_COPY.check;

    text.append(title, check);

    const arrow = document.createElement('span');
    arrow.className = 'review-strip__arrow';
    arrow.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    arrow.textContent = '→';

    link.append(text, arrow);
    item.append(link);

    // The replay, when there is one. Plain text in a <p>: the reader's sentence
    // is neither edited nor truncated in the DOM — the three-line clamp is CSS,
    // so a screen reader still reads the whole note, and following the card
    // reaches the box that holds it in full.
    const note = readNote(lesson.slug);
    if (note) {
      const wrote = document.createElement('p');
      wrote.className = 'review-strip__wrote';
      wrote.setAttribute('data-review-note', lesson.slug);
      wrote.textContent = `${REVIEW_COPY.wrote} ${note}`;
      item.append(wrote);
    }
    return item;
  }

  /** The whole strip for a non-empty queue. */
  function build(due: LessonRef[]): HTMLElement {
    const strip = document.createElement('section');
    strip.className = 'review-strip';
    strip.setAttribute('data-review-strip', '');
    strip.setAttribute('aria-labelledby', HEADING_ID);

    const heading = document.createElement('h2');
    heading.className = 'review-strip__heading';
    heading.id = HEADING_ID;
    heading.textContent = REVIEW_COPY.heading;

    const note = document.createElement('p');
    note.className = 'review-strip__note';
    note.textContent = REVIEW_COPY.note;

    const list = document.createElement('ul');
    list.className = 'review-strip__list';
    // Explicit `role`: Safari drops list semantics from a `list-style: none`
    // list, and the count of what is on offer is the one thing this strip
    // genuinely owes a screen-reader user.
    list.setAttribute('role', 'list');
    for (const lesson of due) list.append(card(lesson));

    strip.append(heading, note, list);
    return strip;
  }

  /**
   * Redraws the strip from storage — or removes it, or does nothing at all.
   *
   * Nothing due means no nodes: not a hidden container, not an empty state.
   */
  function render(): void {
    const host = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-lessons]');
    if (!host) return; // not `/learn` (or no injected list): leave the page alone
    const due = selectDueReviews(parseLessonRefs(host.dataset['lessons']));
    // The note is part of the signature, not just the slug: a reader who writes
    // or deletes one on a lesson page and comes Back lands on this page from the
    // back/forward cache, where `rendered` still holds what was drawn before
    // they left — so a slug-only signature would leave their old words (or none)
    // quoted under the card. At most two due lessons, so at most two reads.
    const signature = due
      .map((lesson) => `${lesson.slug}\n${readNote(lesson.slug) ?? ''}`)
      .join('\n');

    const existing = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-review-strip]');
    if (existing && signature === rendered) return;
    existing?.remove();
    rendered = signature;
    if (due.length === 0) return;

    host.insertAdjacentElement('afterend', build(due));
  }

  render();
  // A back/forward-cache restore replays no script, so a reader who reviewed a
  // lesson and came Back would still be invited to review it. (`astro:page-load`
  // covers View Transitions if a ClientRouter is ever added; none is installed
  // today, so it is the pageshow hook that actually fires.)
  onRestore(render);
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', render);
  // The reset control lives in `/learn`'s own island and deletes the records
  // this strip is derived from. Nothing else would tell this one, and a strip
  // left standing after a reset would invite the reader to review a lesson the
  // device has just forgotten.
  document.addEventListener(PROGRESS_RESET_EVENT, render);
</script>
